Return-Path: bnite@tremere.ios.com
Received: from tremere.ios.com (tremere.ios.com [169.132.32.250]) by marco.internetforce.com (8.6.12/8.6.9) with ESMTP id PAA16270 for <ianna@marco.internetforce.com>; Wed, 11 Sep 1996 15:44:25 +0100
Received: from localhost (bnite@localhost) by tremere.ios.com (8.7.5/8.6.9) with SMTP id KAA15104 for <ianna@marco.internetforce.com>; Wed, 11 Sep 1996 10:37:04 -0400 (EDT)
Date: Wed, 11 Sep 1996 10:37:03 -0400 (EDT)
From: Golan Ben-Oni <bnite@tremere.ios.com>
To: Marco Iannacone <ianna@marco.internetforce.com>
Subject: Re: routing of some class C (2)
In-Reply-To: <Pine.LNX.3.91.960911152136.16002C-100000@marco.internetforce.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.SUN.3.95.960911102502.14579B-100000@tremere.ios.com>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=US-ASCII
Status: RO
X-Status: A

> Hi Golan,
> 
> sorry if I bother you again...
> 
> my previous question was:
> 
> Can IDT do the propagation?

Yes, however keep in mind the following:

It has been widely known that for some time Sprint Corporation
has been using filters which limit the amount of routes that
get propagated throughout their network.  Their filters basically
do not ensure routing from any network smaller than a /18.  Since this
block is smaller (only 4 class C's) we too can not ensure that it will
be routed to by everyone.

This is also the answer we give to our customers who want to come onto the
internet with their own class C's assigned to them by the internic.

The best way to ensure that your customers will always be routed would be
to assign them from network addresses from a block that is large in size.

> How much does it costs?

This is free.

> How long it will take?

This can take anywhere from several hours to a day depending on how
quickly we can get other providers (such as Alternet) to make changes to
their access-lists ...

